    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Could you go into a bit more detail about the chemical mechanism? Like, what exactly is happening at the molecular level with the iron and manganese when the pH climbs above 7.0 that makes them unavailable to the roots? I want to make sure the report sounds sufficiently technical for the owner.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When the soil pH climbs above 7.0, the chemical mechanism shifts toward precipitation, which is the root of the problem for iron and manganese. At higher pH levels, the concentration of hydroxide ions ($\\text{OH}^-$) increases, and these ions react with the dissolved metal ions ($\\text{Fe}^{2+}$, $\\text{Fe}^{3+}$, $\\text{Mn}^{2+}$) to form insoluble hydroxides. For instance, iron readily precipitates as ferric hydroxide ($\\text{Fe}(\\text{OH})_3$), which is practically unavailable for root uptake. This process effectively sequesters the micronutrients into solid, non-soluble forms, making them biochemically inaccessible to the vine's root system.",
      "source": "target",
